Practice 6 grammar (EBNF): structs, functions, field access and calls on
top of the Practice 5 language (if/else, while, blocks, !).

Notation: | alternatives, [ x ] optional, { x } zero or more,
"quoted" terminals are token kinds the lexer emits verbatim,
unquoted names are non-terminals with a rule below.

program    ::= { struct } { fn } { statement } exit_stmt
struct     ::= "struct" ident NL "{" NL field_decl { field_decl } "}" NL
field_decl ::= type [ "mut" ] ident NL
fn         ::= "fn" ident ":=" "(" [ type ident { "," type ident } ] ")"
               "->" type NL block
statement  ::= decl | assign | if | while
decl       ::= type [ "mut" ] ident "{" init_value { "," init_value } "}"
init_value ::= expr | "{" init_value { "," init_value } "}"
type       ::= "i32" | "i64" | "bool" | ident
assign     ::= ident { "." ident } ":=" expr
if         ::= "if" expr NL block [ "else" NL block ]
while      ::= "while" expr NL block
block      ::= "{" NL { statement } [ exit_stmt NL ] "}" NL   (not empty)
exit_stmt  ::= "exit" factor
expr       ::= arith [ ( "==" | "!=" ) arith ]
arith      ::= term { ( "+" | "-" ) term }
term       ::= factor { "*" factor }
factor     ::= number | "true" | "false" | ident | field | call | "!" factor
field      ::= ident "." ident { "." ident }
call       ::= ident "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"

Terminals ident and number come from the lexer as "identifier" and
"constant" tokens; how they are spelled is the lexer's rule, not the
grammar's. "struct", "fn", "i32", "i64", "bool", "mut", "exit", "true",
"false", "if", "else", "while" are the lexer's keywords, "{"/"}" block
tokens, "("/")" their own token kind (so a call's parentheses never read
as a block), ":="/"->"/"=="/"!="/"!"/"."/"," operator tokens, "+"/"-"/"*"
single-byte operator tokens, used here with the text they carry. NL is
the end of a line: one statement is one line, except if/while/struct/
fn/block, which are several - the parser keeps a line cursor next to
its token cursor for exactly this reason.

A struct's type is a built-in type or an ident naming a struct declared
above it; the grammar does not distinguish a struct type from any other
ident - that a given name was actually declared, and what it names, is
semantics, not syntax (the parser dispatches a line starting with ident
ident... to decl the same way it dispatches "i32 x..."). Likewise a
field/call's ident is any identifier; whether it resolves to a known
function or a known field of the right type is checker.py's job.

init_value's recursion ("{" init_value { "," init_value } "}" standing
in for a single value) is the additional task: a struct field's value
may itself be written as a brace list instead of a variable or a call,
built field by field, nested as deep as the struct nests. decl's own
outer "{" ... "}" is the same rule one level up - a decl is just an
init_value with a name and a type in front of it.

exit_stmt still takes one operand: a constant, a variable, a field
chain or a call, never an operation - "exit" calls parse_factor, not
parse_expr, exactly as in Practice 5. A call alone, with no "exit" and
no ":=" catching its result, is not a statement; the field/call
alternatives of factor cover every other place a value is needed.

Semantics the grammar cannot express live in checker.py, not here:
- a stack of scope frames, one per block (Practice 5), now swapped for
  a fresh one holding just the parameters when checking a function body
  - the body sees its own parameters and locals and every function of
  the program (by name, regardless of declaration order - signatures
  are all collected before any body is checked), never the top-level
  variables;
- a struct's fields are checked against the structs declared strictly
  above it, so a struct can never contain itself, directly or through
  another struct - this falls out of the ordering, not a separate
  occurs-check;
- a decl/call's values and arguments are checked field by field /
  parameter by parameter against the type they fill, with the same
  i32-into-i64 widening rule as Practice 4, and a single value of a
  struct's own type copies the whole object instead of filling its
  first field;
- a field is constant unless its declaration says mut; "x.y.z := e"
  needs x itself mut and every field named on the way (y, z) mut - one
  missing mut anywhere on the chain rejects the whole assignment, even
  if the variable and the final field are both mut; replacing a whole
  object with ":=" additionally needs every field of its type, at
  every depth, to be mut (a single non-mut field blocks ":=" on the
  whole object even when the variable holding it is mut);
- inside a function exit's value must match the function's declared
  result type (with the same widening rule); at the top level it still
  has no target type to match, only the rule that it must be built-in,
  never a struct.
That a call's arguments are evaluated left to right, and that an inner
"{}" is built depth-first before the object that holds it, is not
semantics either: it falls out of a single recursive-descent walk, the
same way operator precedence did in Practice 4/5.
